    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that's really strong. i like how you called it 'antagonistic to true wildness'. can you add a bit more about why the managed approach is basically just a failure to understand modern ecology? like, why is it so fundamentally broken compared to rewilding?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The fundamental break in the managed approach is that it often applies 19th-century, simplistic views of \"balance\" to a complex, dynamic 21st-century world of modern ecology. True nature is inherently unpredictable and self-correcting, not a carefully curated exhibit that needs constant human propping up to \"succeed.\" In contrast, rewilding embraces this inherent dynamism, favoring the powerful, often radical, success of natural system self-regulation over the predictable but often artificial stability of human control.",
      "source": "target",
